        <h2>Day Trips from Mawella</h2>
        <p><a href="/places/tangalle">Tangalle</a> is 7km east — a small town with excellent seafood restaurants and its own beautiful, wind-swept beach. The drive or tuk-tuk along the coast road passes through several small villages and is scenic in its own right. <a href="/places/hiriketiya">Hiriketiya Beach</a> — the photogenic bowl-shaped cove beloved by surfers and photographers — is about 15km west, roughly 25 minutes by tuk-tuk, and well worth a half-day visit.</p>
        <p>Mulkirigala Rock Temple, about 16km north of Mawella, is one of the south's most impressive ancient sites — a series of cave temples carved into a 200-metre granite outcrop, with reclining Buddha images and painted ceilings that date back over 2,000 years. Far fewer visitors come here than to Dambulla or Sigiriya, making it one of the most atmospheric temple visits in the country. Combine it with a morning at Mawella Bay for a memorable full day.</p>

        <h2>Getting to Mawella Bay</h2>
        <p>There is no train station at Mawella. The most practical approach is to take a train or bus to <a href="/places/tangalle">Tangalle</a> and then hire a tuk-tuk for the 7km journey west (LKR 400–600). Alternatively, many guesthouses in Tangalle will arrange a tuk-tuk for the day. From Colombo, the Southern Expressway to Matara takes about 2.5 hours, then the A2 coastal road continues east to Tangalle. From <a href="/places/mirissa">Mirissa</a>, allow 1.5 hours by road. From <a href="/places/yala">Yala</a>, Tangalle is the nearest major town — about 1.5 hours by car.</p>

